description:
`groupSlices` (`pkg-js/src/components/km.ts:171-187`, `:241-245`) treats each group as one block from its first row to its last, so rows of other groups in between become part of the curve (drawn at `:385-397`). The tooltip (`:292-305`) reads the same slices. The R and Python docs only ask for rows to be ascending within a group, so a table sorted by time overall meets the documented rule.

```js
createKm(el, {
  data: {
    columns: {
      time: [0, 0, 5, 7, 12, 15],
      surv: [1, 1, 0.9, 0.8, 0.7, 0.5],
      group: ["T", "C", "T", "C", "T", "C"],
    },
  },
});
```

The T curve is drawn through (0, 1), (5, 0.9), (7, 0.8), (12, 0.7), and (7, 0.8) belongs to C. The C curve picks up T points the same way.

## Fix

Collect each group's rows by key (a stable sort by group, then time) before drawing and hit-testing.
